Ladybird reads the stream of a request’s body to its end when fetch()
sends the body, even when it sends the bytes from the body’s source;
so, a tee of that stream (a clone of the Request) finishes reading, and
the request’s body is used afterward. The other engines all consume
the stream as well. Blink’s FetchManager takes the bytes through
BodyStreamBuffer::DrainAsFormData(), which closes, locks, and disturbs
the stream. Gecko’s FetchRequest() calls FetchBody::SetBodyUsed(),
which locks the stream and starts its FetchStreamReader consuming it.
And WebKit’s FetchResponse::fetch() reads a request’s exposed body
stream to an ArrayBuffer, with convertReadableStreamToArrayBuffer(),
before it starts the load.
